1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,450
On Sunday morning, the fourth of April

2
00:00:02,470 --> 00:00:05,487
1943, the prisoners of the Davao Penal

3
00:00:05,507 --> 00:00:07,510
Colony, on the Philippine island of

4
00:00:07,530 --> 00:00:10,235
Mindanao, lined up for the day's work.

5
00:00:10,255 --> 00:00:13,068
One by one the work details marched out

6
00:00:13,088 --> 00:00:15,547
through the gate, while a Japanese guard

7
00:00:15,567 --> 00:00:17,672
checked them off on a chalkboard.

8
00:00:17,692 --> 00:00:20,151
Among them walked ten Americans whose bags

9
00:00:20,171 --> 00:00:22,276
were packed a little too full.

10
00:00:22,296 --> 00:00:24,755
Across the compound, a friend shouted at

11
00:00:24,775 --> 00:00:25,818
one of them.

12
00:00:25,838 --> 00:00:27,943
Hey, Steve, your toothbrush is sticking

13
00:00:27,963 --> 00:00:29,359
out of your bag.

14
00:00:29,379 --> 00:00:31,150
Are you planning to escape?

15
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:33,293
He was.

16
00:00:33,313 --> 00:00:36,139
That evening, at the six o'clock roll

17
00:00:36,159 --> 00:00:38,578
call, the guards counted the prisoners.

18
00:00:38,598 --> 00:00:40,610
Then they counted them again,

19
00:00:40,630 --> 00:00:41,423
and again.

20
00:00:41,443 --> 00:00:43,863
Ten Americans and two Filipino convicts

21
00:00:43,883 --> 00:00:44,676
were gone.

22
00:00:44,696 --> 00:00:47,928
In the ten years the penal colony had

23
00:00:47,948 --> 00:00:51,200
existed, no prisoner had ever escaped from it.

24
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:54,765
This is the documented story of the only

25
00:00:54,785 --> 00:00:56,591
successful group escape from a Japanese

26
00:00:56,611 --> 00:00:58,417
prison camp in the Pacific war:

27
00:00:58,437 --> 00:01:01,156
ten men who walked into a swamp to tell

28
00:01:01,176 --> 00:01:02,982
America about the Bataan Death March,

29
00:01:03,002 --> 00:01:05,113
and the months their own government then

30
00:01:05,133 --> 00:01:06,350
made them keep quiet.

31
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:10,921
Most of them had been prisoners for a year.

32
00:01:10,941 --> 00:01:14,060
On the ninth of April 1942,

33
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:16,803
the starving American and Filipino army on

34
00:01:16,823 --> 00:01:19,546
the Bataan peninsula surrendered to the Japanese.

35
00:01:19,566 --> 00:01:22,289
Its men were marched north for roughly

36
00:01:22,309 --> 00:01:24,640
sixty-five miles in the tropical heat.

37
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:26,207
Nearly seven hundred Americans,

38
00:01:26,227 --> 00:01:28,950
and perhaps as many as ten thousand

39
00:01:28,970 --> 00:01:31,693
Filipinos, are believed to have died on

40
00:01:31,713 --> 00:01:32,477
the way.

41
00:01:32,497 --> 00:01:34,436
The prisoners called it, simply,

42
00:01:34,456 --> 00:01:35,256
the Hike.

43
00:01:36,575 --> 00:01:39,479
At the end of it was Camp O'Donnell.

44
00:01:39,499 --> 00:01:41,672
Of about nine thousand Americans who

45
00:01:41,692 --> 00:01:43,865
walked through its gate in April,

46
00:01:43,885 --> 00:01:46,424
nearly two thousand were dead by June.

47
00:01:46,444 --> 00:01:49,295
Then came Cabanatuan.

48
00:01:49,315 --> 00:01:52,285
In November 1942, about two thousand

49
00:01:52,305 --> 00:01:54,495
prisoners were shipped south to Mindanao,

50
00:01:54,515 --> 00:01:56,637
to a prison farm cut out of the jungle

51
00:01:56,657 --> 00:01:58,375
near Davao.

52
00:01:58,395 --> 00:02:00,089
The Japanese commandant looked them over

53
00:02:00,109 --> 00:02:02,661
and said he had asked for workers and been

54
00:02:02,681 --> 00:02:04,395
sent a batch of walking corpses.

55
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:07,696
Ed Dyess was one of them.

56
00:02:07,716 --> 00:02:09,858
He was a fighter pilot from Albany,

57
00:02:09,878 --> 00:02:12,720
Texas, who had led a squadron of P-forties

58
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:14,858
on Bataan until there were almost no

59
00:02:14,878 --> 00:02:17,302
planes left, and then fought on the ground

60
00:02:17,322 --> 00:02:18,524
as an infantry officer.

61
00:02:18,544 --> 00:02:20,967
He was captured on the day of the

62
00:02:20,987 --> 00:02:22,820
surrender and walked the whole march.

63
00:02:24,550 --> 00:02:27,120
The others came from every service.

64
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:30,573
Melvyn McCoy was a Navy officer who had

65
00:02:30,593 --> 00:02:33,594
left Annapolis with one of the best

66
00:02:33,614 --> 00:02:36,184
mathematics records the academy had seen.

67
00:02:36,204 --> 00:02:37,478
He would navigate.

68
00:02:37,498 --> 00:02:41,000
Stephen Mellnik was an Army major from Corregidor.

69
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:43,670
Austin Shofner, Jack Hawkins and Mike

70
00:02:43,690 --> 00:02:45,583
Dobervich were Marines.

71
00:02:45,603 --> 00:02:49,052
Sam Grashio was a pilot and Leo Boelens a

72
00:02:49,072 --> 00:02:50,898
mechanic, both Army airmen.

73
00:02:50,918 --> 00:02:54,150
Paul Marshall and Bob Spielman were sergeants.

74
00:02:55,350 --> 00:02:57,690
Two Filipinos completed the party.

75
00:02:57,710 --> 00:03:01,114
Benigno de la Cruz and Victorio Jumarong

76
00:03:01,134 --> 00:03:02,834
were convicts at the colony,

77
00:03:02,854 --> 00:03:04,554
both sent there for killings.

78
00:03:04,574 --> 00:03:06,962
Jumarong had once helped clear a trail

79
00:03:06,982 --> 00:03:11,250
through the jungle to a village called Lungaog.

80
00:03:11,270 --> 00:03:13,390
Without that trail, the plan was hopeless.

81
00:03:14,750 --> 00:03:18,168
The plan was dangerous for more than the ten.

82
00:03:18,188 --> 00:03:21,421
At Cabanatuan, the Japanese had grouped

83
00:03:21,441 --> 00:03:24,100
the prisoners into shooting squads of ten.

84
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:27,545
If one man escaped, the others could be shot.

85
00:03:27,565 --> 00:03:30,323
Nobody at Davao knew for certain whether

86
00:03:30,343 --> 00:03:31,997
the rule still applied.

87
00:03:32,017 --> 00:03:33,670
The ten went anyway.

88
00:03:33,690 --> 00:03:36,985
Mellnik put the reason simply.

89
00:03:37,005 --> 00:03:39,750
The horrors of O'Donnell, Cabanatuan and

90
00:03:39,770 --> 00:03:42,030
Davao had to become a matter of record.

91
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:46,187
For weeks they had smuggled food and

92
00:03:46,207 --> 00:03:48,938
supplies out to the fields and buried them.

93
00:03:48,958 --> 00:03:51,689
On the morning of the escape they slipped

94
00:03:51,709 --> 00:03:54,096
away from their work details into a

95
00:03:54,116 --> 00:03:56,503
coconut grove and dug up their bundles,

96
00:03:56,523 --> 00:03:58,566
soaked by a week of rain.

97
00:03:58,586 --> 00:04:00,973
Then they waited for the two Filipinos.

98
00:04:00,993 --> 00:04:02,692
Ten minutes passed, then twenty,

99
00:04:02,712 --> 00:04:04,067
and the sky opened.

100
00:04:04,087 --> 00:04:06,474
At nine o'clock, Jumarong walked out of

101
00:04:06,494 --> 00:04:09,569
the rain alone, with a question from his friend.

102
00:04:09,589 --> 00:04:12,340
Ben wanted to know if they were ready.

103
00:04:13,625 --> 00:04:14,948
Then came the swamp.

104
00:04:14,968 --> 00:04:16,962
The water rose to their knees,

105
00:04:16,982 --> 00:04:18,976
and sharp-edged grass grew higher than

106
00:04:18,996 --> 00:04:19,647
their heads.

107
00:04:19,667 --> 00:04:21,997
The first night they camped where the

108
00:04:22,017 --> 00:04:23,675
water was only ankle deep,

109
00:04:23,695 --> 00:04:26,360
and woke up half floating in their beds.

110
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:29,381
In places they waded in water as deep as

111
00:04:29,401 --> 00:04:32,067
their armpits, while McCoy kept them on a

112
00:04:32,087 --> 00:04:33,765
compass bearing to the northeast.

113
00:04:35,225 --> 00:04:38,115
On the fourth day, walking along an old

114
00:04:38,135 --> 00:04:40,297
railroad, they met a fifteen-year-old boy,

115
00:04:40,317 --> 00:04:43,207
the first human being they had seen since

116
00:04:43,227 --> 00:04:43,934
the escape.

117
00:04:43,954 --> 00:04:46,480
He ran, then stopped when the Filipinos

118
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:47,935
called out to him.

119
00:04:47,955 --> 00:04:50,118
His village, he said, was close.

120
00:04:50,138 --> 00:04:52,275
It was Lungaog.

121
00:04:52,975 --> 00:04:54,853
The whole village turned out.

122
00:04:54,873 --> 00:04:57,511
The Americans ate boiled corn and chicken,

123
00:04:57,531 --> 00:05:00,928
and a guerrilla played La Paloma on a mandolin.

124
00:05:00,948 --> 00:05:03,206
Days later, at a sugar plantation,

125
00:05:03,226 --> 00:05:06,244
Austin Shofner wrote in his diary that he

126
00:05:06,264 --> 00:05:08,901
was listening to a phonograph play the

127
00:05:08,921 --> 00:05:11,939
first American music he had heard in a

128
00:05:11,959 --> 00:05:12,698
long time.

129
00:05:12,718 --> 00:05:14,976
Not every meeting went so well.

130
00:05:14,996 --> 00:05:17,297
One guerrilla mistook Dyess for a Japanese

131
00:05:17,317 --> 00:05:19,775
soldier, took careful aim and pulled the trigger.

132
00:05:21,015 --> 00:05:21,915
Dyess survived it.

133
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:25,636
Guerrilla bands passed them from one to

134
00:05:25,656 --> 00:05:27,792
the next across some three hundred miles

135
00:05:27,812 --> 00:05:29,540
of Mindanao.

136
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:31,960
On the ninth of July 1943,

137
00:05:31,980 --> 00:05:33,580
the submarine Trout took Dyess,

138
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:35,946
McCoy and Mellnik off the island.

139
00:05:35,966 --> 00:05:38,950
Most of the others followed later by submarine.

140
00:05:38,970 --> 00:05:41,578
Three stayed on to fight with the

141
00:05:41,598 --> 00:05:43,455
guerrillas, and one of them,

142
00:05:43,475 --> 00:05:46,354
Leo Boelens, was killed by the Japanese in

143
00:05:46,374 --> 00:05:48,350
January 1944.

144
00:05:49,050 --> 00:05:51,857
In Australia they told their story to

145
00:05:51,877 --> 00:05:53,068
General Douglas MacArthur.

146
00:05:53,088 --> 00:05:56,299
The Japanese, he said, will pay for that

147
00:05:56,319 --> 00:05:57,530
humiliation and suffering.

148
00:05:58,750 --> 00:06:01,253
Dyess's account was typed up and flown to

149
00:06:01,273 --> 00:06:01,577
Washington.

150
00:06:01,597 --> 00:06:04,163
The War Department would later call it the

151
00:06:04,183 --> 00:06:06,770
greatest story of the war in the Pacific.

152
00:06:08,150 --> 00:06:09,590
And then, nothing happened.

153
00:06:10,925 --> 00:06:13,488
If you want more prisoner of war stories

154
00:06:13,508 --> 00:06:16,205
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

155
00:06:16,225 --> 00:06:16,674
Frontlines.

156
00:06:16,694 --> 00:06:19,505
It helps us keep finding them.

157
00:06:21,125 --> 00:06:23,425
Ed Dyess landed in the United States on

158
00:06:23,445 --> 00:06:26,460
the ninth of August 1943, his

159
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:27,251
twenty-seventh birthday.

160
00:06:27,271 --> 00:06:28,438
Nobody met him.

161
00:06:28,458 --> 00:06:31,206
There were no reporters and no photographers.

162
00:06:31,226 --> 00:06:34,369
Officially, he was not even in the country.

163
00:06:34,389 --> 00:06:37,533
He gave a long statement to the War

164
00:06:37,553 --> 00:06:40,301
Department and ended it with a plea.

165
00:06:40,321 --> 00:06:41,883
It was absolutely necessary,

166
00:06:41,903 --> 00:06:45,046
he said, that all civilized people of the

167
00:06:45,066 --> 00:06:47,814
world know the conditions of the Japanese

168
00:06:47,834 --> 00:06:48,634
prison camps.

169
00:06:49,875 --> 00:06:52,508
Instead, he was sent to Ashford General

170
00:06:52,528 --> 00:06:54,024
Hospital in West Virginia,

171
00:06:54,044 --> 00:06:56,298
a luxury resort before the war,

172
00:06:56,318 --> 00:06:58,194
and ordered to keep quiet.

173
00:06:58,214 --> 00:07:01,226
If he told his story to anyone without

174
00:07:01,246 --> 00:07:03,500
permission, he risked his commission and

175
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:04,637
possible criminal charges.

176
00:07:04,657 --> 00:07:07,375
McCoy and Mellnik were silenced the same way.

177
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:10,961
The order came from the top.

178
00:07:10,981 --> 00:07:12,946
President Roosevelt agreed that publishing

179
00:07:12,966 --> 00:07:14,930
Japanese atrocities could endanger the

180
00:07:14,950 --> 00:07:17,311
Gripsholm, a neutral Swedish ship carrying

181
00:07:17,331 --> 00:07:19,693
food and medicine for Allied prisoners,

182
00:07:19,713 --> 00:07:22,074
and could make their treatment worse.

183
00:07:22,094 --> 00:07:24,852
He asked that no story from escaped

184
00:07:24,872 --> 00:07:27,630
prisoners be published until he authorized it.

185
00:07:27,650 --> 00:07:30,805
Some historians think there was more to it.

186
00:07:30,825 --> 00:07:33,981
A story about the men left behind on

187
00:07:34,001 --> 00:07:36,362
Bataan raised awkward questions about how

188
00:07:36,382 --> 00:07:39,160
little Washington had sent to the Pacific.

189
00:07:40,575 --> 00:07:43,884
The Chicago Tribune wanted the story anyway.

190
00:07:43,904 --> 00:07:45,850
It offered Dyess twenty-one thousand

191
00:07:45,870 --> 00:07:47,788
dollars and sent a reporter,

192
00:07:47,808 --> 00:07:50,027
Charles Leavelle, to his hospital room,

193
00:07:50,047 --> 00:07:52,639
where Dyess dictated his story from his bed.

194
00:07:52,659 --> 00:07:54,978
The finished chapters went to the War

195
00:07:54,998 --> 00:07:57,650
Department, and the War Department sat on them.

196
00:07:57,670 --> 00:07:59,320
Nothing was going to happen,

197
00:07:59,340 --> 00:08:01,325
an Army general told the paper,

198
00:08:01,345 --> 00:08:03,015
for at least six weeks.

199
00:08:04,325 --> 00:08:06,424
Dyess was allowed home to Albany,

200
00:08:06,444 --> 00:08:09,080
Texas, for six days, with Army escorts at

201
00:08:09,100 --> 00:08:10,739
his side the whole time.

202
00:08:10,759 --> 00:08:13,394
The newspapers could not print what he had

203
00:08:13,414 --> 00:08:16,049
seen, so they wrote about what he ate.

204
00:08:16,069 --> 00:08:17,065
One headline read:

205
00:08:17,505 --> 00:08:19,690
One-Man Scourge of Japanese Turns Talents

206
00:08:19,710 --> 00:08:20,745
on Texas Steak.

207
00:08:22,425 --> 00:08:24,615
Then he went back to flying.

208
00:08:24,635 --> 00:08:26,457
On Wednesday, the twenty-second of

209
00:08:26,477 --> 00:08:29,265
December 1943, Dyess took off in a

210
00:08:29,285 --> 00:08:31,965
P-thirty-eight fighter from Grand Central

211
00:08:31,985 --> 00:08:34,005
Air Terminal, near Burbank,

212
00:08:34,025 --> 00:08:34,515
California.

213
00:08:34,535 --> 00:08:38,085
Just after takeoff, one engine caught fire.

214
00:08:38,105 --> 00:08:40,145
Below him were houses.

215
00:08:41,525 --> 00:08:43,388
He stayed with the burning plane.

216
00:08:43,408 --> 00:08:45,584
He brought it down toward a street,

217
00:08:45,604 --> 00:08:48,408
lifted it over a car that pulled out in

218
00:08:48,428 --> 00:08:50,918
front of him, and banked for a small

219
00:08:50,938 --> 00:08:51,545
vacant lot.

220
00:08:51,565 --> 00:08:54,055
A wing clipped the steeple of a church,

221
00:08:54,075 --> 00:08:56,251
and the plane cartwheeled into the lot.

222
00:08:56,271 --> 00:08:58,213
Ed Dyess was killed.

223
00:08:58,233 --> 00:09:00,225
He was twenty-seven.

224
00:09:00,925 --> 00:09:03,363
Five days later, in a cold rain,

225
00:09:03,383 --> 00:09:05,822
several hundred people filled a church in

226
00:09:05,842 --> 00:09:07,227
Albany for his memorial.

227
00:09:07,247 --> 00:09:09,685
A general told them he would recommend

228
00:09:09,705 --> 00:09:11,605
Dyess for the Medal of Honor.

229
00:09:11,625 --> 00:09:14,485
The story he had come home to tell had

230
00:09:14,505 --> 00:09:15,785
still not been printed.

231
00:09:17,525 --> 00:09:20,575
A month later, the government changed its mind.

232
00:09:20,595 --> 00:09:22,877
At midnight on Friday, the twenty-eighth

233
00:09:22,897 --> 00:09:26,714
of January 1944, the Army and Navy

234
00:09:26,734 --> 00:09:29,216
released a joint statement on Japanese

235
00:09:29,236 --> 00:09:30,885
atrocities against American prisoners.

236
00:09:30,905 --> 00:09:34,222
The New York Times headline ran across the

237
00:09:34,242 --> 00:09:35,056
front page:

238
00:09:35,076 --> 00:09:37,142
five thousand two hundred Americans,

239
00:09:37,162 --> 00:09:39,644
and many more Filipinos, dead of

240
00:09:39,664 --> 00:09:41,750
starvation and torture after Bataan.

241
00:09:42,450 --> 00:09:44,510
That Sunday, the first part of the Dyess

242
00:09:44,530 --> 00:09:47,674
story ran on the front page of the Chicago

243
00:09:47,694 --> 00:09:50,486
Tribune and in about a hundred other newspapers.

244
00:09:50,506 --> 00:09:52,243
There would be twenty-four installments,

245
00:09:52,263 --> 00:09:55,055
one a day for most of a month.

246
00:09:55,075 --> 00:09:56,812
On the seventh of February,

247
00:09:56,832 --> 00:09:59,170
Life magazine printed McCoy and Mellnik's

248
00:09:59,190 --> 00:10:00,882
account across fifteen pages,

249
00:10:00,902 --> 00:10:03,470
with photographs of all ten escapees.

250
00:10:04,675 --> 00:10:06,062
The country was furious.

251
00:10:06,082 --> 00:10:08,172
At the launching of the battleship

252
00:10:08,192 --> 00:10:09,227
Missouri, Margaret Truman,

253
00:10:09,247 --> 00:10:11,337
the senator's daughter who christened her,

254
00:10:11,357 --> 00:10:14,150
said she hoped the ship would be an

255
00:10:14,170 --> 00:10:16,260
avenger for the heroes of Bataan.

256
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:17,667
War bond sales soared.

257
00:10:17,687 --> 00:10:20,128
From that day on, one historian writes,

258
00:10:20,148 --> 00:10:23,293
the nature of the war in the Pacific was

259
00:10:23,313 --> 00:10:25,775
drilled into the minds of all Americans.

260
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:30,163
The men left behind at Davao were not

261
00:10:30,183 --> 00:10:31,809
saved by the story.

262
00:10:31,829 --> 00:10:35,240
In 1944, the last American prisoners from

263
00:10:35,260 --> 00:10:37,648
the colony were loaded onto a Japanese

264
00:10:37,668 --> 00:10:40,160
freighter, the Shinyo Maru.

265
00:10:40,180 --> 00:10:41,845
On the seventh of September,

266
00:10:41,865 --> 00:10:43,529
an American submarine, the Paddle,

267
00:10:43,549 --> 00:10:46,100
torpedoed it off Mindanao.

268
00:10:46,120 --> 00:10:49,167
Its commander had no idea the ship was

269
00:10:49,187 --> 00:10:50,317
carrying American prisoners.

270
00:10:50,337 --> 00:10:53,020
Fewer than a hundred of them survived.

271
00:10:54,350 --> 00:10:57,122
Of the ten, Sam Grashio stayed in the Air

272
00:10:57,142 --> 00:11:01,010
Force and retired a colonel in 1965.

